Farmers formed cooperatives, borrowed from the new Rural Electri-
fication  Administration  at  2  to  3  percent,  and  wired  themselves.
Within twenty to twenty-five years virtually all of rural America had
power, and almost every loan was repaid; the default rate was under 1
percent.  Federal  Reserve  Bank  of  Richmond,  Electrifying  Rural
America Those  cooperatives  still  serve  an  estimated  42  million
people. Americaʼs Electric Cooperatives, Fact Sheet (2026)
Thatʼs the pattern for everything this book wants machines to do.
When the market wonʼt serve a need because the need doesnʼt pay,
people  can  own  the  infrastructure  themselves,  with  public  credit
behind them.
Who. Co-ops, credit unions, cities, and the builders in Chapters 13
and 17.
How youʼll know. The share of the floorʼs capacity (kitchens, growing
space, compute, delivery) that the community owns.
Rung 6: Stop letting a score decide who survives
What. Take money tests out of essentials. A lawyer for every tenant
facing eviction. Limits on using credit reports and private risk scores
to decide who gets housing and utilities. No shutoffs that leave people
without water, power, or heat.
Proof itʼs possible. In 2017, New York City became the first city in the
country to establish a right to counsel for tenants facing eviction. The
law provided full representation to households at or below 200 per-
cent of the federal poverty guidelines and brief legal assistance above
that threshold. In the 2022 fiscal year, 78 percent of households rep-
resented in court by those lawyers kept their homes. This is a prece-
dent for public legal help, not proof that income tests have already
disappeared. NYC Office of Civil Justice, Annual Report 2022
Who. Tenant unions, legal aid groups, city councils, state legislatures.
How  youʼll  know. Eviction  filings,  lockouts,  and  shutoffs,  counted
and published, falling.
